\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\usepackage{float}

\title{Riverside City Marathon\\Race-Day Volunteer Handbook}
\author{Volunteer Coordination Office}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle



\section{Overview}
Welcome to the 2026 Riverside City Marathon race-day team. Roughly 900
volunteers staff the start pens, the course, and the finish festival.
This handbook collects the operational details every team leader should
review before race morning.

Team leaders pick up radios and signage at the volunteer tent on Friday
evening. Each team confirms its roster by 20:00 so that no-shows can be
backfilled from the standby list overnight.

\section{Race Day Schedule}

\subsection{Start Times}
The wheelchair race starts at 07:45, the elite field at 08:00, and the
mass start follows in four waves between 08:10 and 08:40. Volunteers at
the start line should be in position by 06:30 for the pen build.

\paragraph{Cut-off Times}
The course closes on a rolling 6:30 pace: the halfway cut-off is at
11:15 and the finish line closes at 14:30. Sweep buses follow the final
wave and collect runners who fall behind the cut-off vehicle.



\subsubsection{Course Closure}
Roads reopen progressively behind the sweep convoy. Course marshals
remain at their junctions until the closure vehicle passes and the
police escort confirms the reopening on the course radio net.

\section{Aid Stations}

\paragraph{Locations}
Nine aid stations stand at roughly 4.5~km intervals, with doubled
stations at the halfway mark and at kilometre 35. Each station has water
on the left tables and electrolyte drink on the right.



\subsection{Supplies}
Every station receives a standard pallet: 40 cases of water, 12 cases of
electrolyte mix, 8000 cups, two medical kits, and a sharps bin. Station
captains count remaining stock at 13:00 and radio the logistics desk if
water drops below eight cases.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{4cm}\rule{12cm}{0pt}}
\caption{Layout of a standard aid station with table positions.}
\end{figure}

\section{Safety and Incidents}
Medical tents operate at the start, halfway, and finish. Any runner who
stops for more than two minutes must be approached and asked whether
they need assistance; volunteers never diagnose, they call the medical
dispatcher on channel~3.

\begin{figure}[h!]
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{4cm}\rule{12cm}{0pt}}
\caption{Incident reporting flow from course marshal to dispatcher.}
\end{figure}



Incidents are logged on the paper cards in each team leader's vest and
handed in at the finish tent. The debrief starts at 16:00 in the
volunteer marquee; attendance is required for team leaders.
\end{document}

